CHAPTER 1 – DATA REPRESENTATION
1.1 Number Systems
Binary (Base 2)
• Uses only 0 and 1.
• Each digit in binary is called a bit.
• Binary is used inside computers because hardware (transistors) can only represent two states: ON (1) or OFF (0).
• Example:
• Binary: 1011₂
• Decimal equivalent: 1×8+0×4+1×2+1×1=111×8 + 0×4 + 1×2 + 1×1 = 111×8+0×4+1×2+1×1=11
Decimal (Base 10)
• The number system humans use every day.
• Uses digits 0–9.
• Each place represents a power of 10.
Hexadecimal (Base 16)
• Uses 16 symbols: 0–9 and A–F (where A=10, B=11, ..., F=15).
• Easier for humans to read long binary numbers.
• Example:
• Binary 11111111 → Hex FF
1.2 Conversions Between Number Systems
Binary → Decimal: Multiply each bit by 2ⁿ and add.
Decimal → Binary: Divide by 2, note remainders (read bottom-up).
Binary → Hexadecimal: Group 4 bits → convert each group.
