1.7 Images
• Image made up of pixels (picture elements).
• Each pixel = binary value representing colour.
• Resolution: Number of pixels in width × height.
• Colour Depth: Number of bits per pixel.
• 1 bit → 2 colours
• 8 bits → 256 colours
• 24 bits → 16 million colours
File size (bits) = width × height × colour depth
1.8 Sound Representation
• Sound is an analogue wave → converted to digital by ADC (Analogue-to-Digital Converter).
Sampling: Measure amplitude at fixed time intervals.
Sample rate: number of samples per second (Hz).
Bit depth: number of bits used per sample.
File size = sample rate × bit depth × duration
Higher rate or depth = better quality but larger file.
1.9 Data Compression
Reduces file size.
• Lossless: No data lost (text, software).
• Lossy: Some data removed (audio, image).
💻 CHAPTER 2 – COMMUNICATION AND INTERNET TECHNOLOGIES
2.1 Data Transmission
Data sent between devices using signals.
Serial Transmission
• Bits sent one after another on one wire.
• Slower, but reliable over long distances.
• Example: USB, RS232.
Parallel Transmission
• Multiple bits sent at once (each bit has its own wire).
• Faster but can cause signal skew.
• Used inside computer.
2.2 Transmission Direction
TypeDescriptionExampleSimplexOne directionPrinter outputHalf-duplexBoth ways, but one at a timeWalkie-talkieFull-duplexBoth directions at oncePhone call
2.3 Transmission Errors
Errors can occur due to noise or interference.
Error Detection Methods:
• Parity Bit:
• Even or Odd parity.
• Detects single-bit errors.
• Checksum:
• Sum of data values sent with message.
• ARQ (Automatic Repeat reQuest):
• If error detected, receiver asks to resend.
