💡 CHAPTER 4 – SOFTWARE
4.1 Types of Software
• System Software: Runs the computer.
• Operating system, utility programs, translators.
• Application Software: Performs user tasks.
• Word processor, spreadsheet, browser.
• Custom / Bespoke Software: Designed for specific user.
4.2 Operating System Functions
• File Management: Organises files/folders.
• Memory Management: Allocates memory to programs.
• Security: Manages user access and passwords.
• User Interface: CLI or GUI.
• Device Management: Controls printers, keyboards, etc.
• Processor Management: Handles multitasking and scheduling.
4.3 Utility Software
Helps maintain system performance.
Examples:
• Anti-virus – Removes malware.
• File Compression – Reduces file size.
• Backup Software – Saves copies of data.
• Disk Defragmentation – Organises files for faster access.
• Firewall – Protects against unauthorised access.
4.4 Language Translators
Used to convert program code to machine code.
TypeDescriptionAssemblerConverts assembly → machine codeCompilerTranslates entire program, creates executable fileInterpreterTranslates line by line, good for debugging
4.5 Programming Languages
• High-Level: Uses English-like syntax (Python, C++, Java).
• Low-Level: Closer to hardware (Assembly language).
Advantages of High-Level:
• Easier to read, portable, faster to code.
4.6 Software Licensing
• Freeware: Free to use but not modify.
• Shareware: Trial version.
• Open Source: Code available to edit and share.
• Proprietary: Owned by company, must be purchased.
